well China is fighting back against  President Trump's decision to impose  those massive tariffs on their products  sent to the US and that leaves the  world's two largest economies on the  brink of a major economic conflict  correspondent Jillian Turner looks at  what that possibly could mean US farmers  are increasingly fearful that a  full-fledged trade war with Beijing is  on the horizon that includes Rob  Schaefer a fourth-generation soybean  farmer in Emory Illinois who also serves  on the board of directors of the  American Soybean Association I'm just  trying to make sure that we get it  through to you know the administration  as far as and I think I'm think they  understand it but I just make sure that  they understand how it's affected in the  people out in the Midwest and as far as  you know my bottom line China is now  threatening to impose its own  retaliatory tariffs on up to three  billion dollars worth of US imports and  American farmers caution that such a  move might incentivize competitors of  American companies to step in and grab  their business Brazil and Argentina okay  are willing and eager to take our share  of the Chinese market and once you lose  a customer it's awful hard to get them  back  China's commerce ministry is currently  considering a 15 percent tariff on  American exports to include dried fruit  wine and steel pipes plus a twenty five  percent tariff on pork and recycled  aluminum China is also drafted a list of  128 American products it would target in  the future should the US and China fail  to reach a bilateral trade agreement  we're losing 375 billion dollars with  China it could be five hundred and four  billion depending on the way you count  it's bad but the US isn't just battling  China in the traditional trade sector in  addition to the disputes over hard goods  such as steel and poultry the two sides  are now clashing over the tech sector  over the past two weeks alarming signs  of a major fallout have emerged the  Trump administration blocked China from  buying US tech giant Qualcomm a deal  worth 117 billion dollars and China  subsequently pulled its own  multi-million dollar investment from US  private equity powerhouse Blackstone our  society is dependent upon innovation in  ways that it hasn't been before but the  Chinese are taking the fruits of  innovation and they're stealing it in  ways which are predatory mercantilist  and completely unacceptable this ongoing  us-china tariff dispute underscores  Washington's latest worry that China  will displace America as the world's  innovation hub and that Shanghai or  Shenzhen may soon become the new Silicon  Valley  Brett Gillian thank you
 

